ng <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"burden\" </i></span>, <span style=\"color: rgba(94, 184, 242, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">31.1%</span> of reported significant p-values (p < .05) are fragile (.01 \u2264 p < .05) - i.e., barely fall under the standard significance threshold. This is a much higher rate than the median Psychology paper (median shown in black); relative to other words, papers with <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"burden\" </i></span> are in the top <span style=\"color: rgba(3, 152, 252, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">94<sup>th</sup></span> percentile of fragile p-value percentages (lower = better). <br>Note, strong/weak p-values highly predict replicability.","statement1":"As of 2020-2024, papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"burden\" </i></span> tend to be published in lower impact factor journals (<span style=\"color: rgba(105, 219, 99, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">8<sup>th</sup></span> percentile among all words)  but tend to be cited more (<span style=\"color: rgba(255, 168, 82, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">84<sup>th</sup></span> percentile word). In general, this word is positively associated with university ranking, such that higher-ranked institutions are more likely to publish papers containing <span style=\"color: rgba(0, 0, 0, 1.0); \"><i>\"burden\" </i></span> (<span style=\"color: rgba(170, 117, 250, 1.0); font-weight: bold;\">77<sup>th</sup></span> percentile). "}